zefciu schrieb:
> 
> What are the best classes for the particular conducts?  I put my guesses
> here, but wait for your opinions:
> 
> vegan/vegetarian - monk of course.  Other are ranger and tourist, but
> these have it harder to get intrinsics.  The worst - wizard, healer.

I find that in the mid-to-long term, the main difficulty of
vegan/vegetarian is the difficulty in acquiring intrinsics. The
starting food of rangers and tourists (and orcs) isn't much of an
advantage here.

There are only very few intrinsic-granting vegetarian/vegan foods (j,
b, F monsters and grey ooze and brown pudding are vegetarian iirc;
some of them are even vegan); getting crowned is a _very good_ turn
for non-monk vegans. Classes/species with valuable starting or 'rank'
intrinsics have an advantage here.
I think wizards also have a slight long-term advantage, because they
gain teleport control sometime and have slightly better odds of
gaining the gear for controlled self-polymorph (to eat rings for
intrinsics).

> weaponless - monk again, also ranger  Worst - caveman, priest?

The two classes who use martial arts have a clear advantage: monk and
samurai. "Unarmed combat" is complete and utter junk; even at grand
mastery it performs worse than clonking things over the head with a
large box.
Classes who can get multi-shot combat skills have the option of
finishing the conduct without resorting to bare-handed monster
bashing. _Very_ carefully played wizards can also do it; you'll have
to hide behind your pet a lot before you get a good stack of daggers,
and acquiring the Eye of the Aethiopica helps a lot.

> illiterate = never tried, but I think it goes with hack'n'slash-ness of
> the class.  Worst - except spellcasters I can't imagine tourist without
> E-word.

The big long-term problem is lack of enchantments on all your gear.
The illiterate late-game can get messy if you depend on melee combat:
barbarians tend to have an annoyingly hard time. Valkyries can do
well, because they have a half-damage quest artefact and can hurl
daggers.

Other mild advantages for illiterate play come from quest artefacts:
Tourist - infinite (blessed) recharging, Monk - enlightenment to
identify rings and amulets, Valkyrie - level teleport.

Wizards aren't much worse than others at illiterate play - it goes
against their role, but hurled daggers are still a good and reliable
weapon, and they get their 20k turns use of two spells regardless.
Barbarians are IMO a weaker choice for illiterates, because they're
bad at everything not melee, and lack of enchantments _hurts_ when
exchanging blows with minotaurs, esp. when your quest artefact is the
flimsy Heart of Ahriman (special power: does double regular luckstone
damage when hurled with a sling).

> genoless - wizard and all neutral characters (magicbane against liches)

A very, very, very tiny advantage. Major liches are _always_ an
exasperating nuisance, worst of all on non-teleport levels.

> polyselfless, polypileless, wishless - don't know.  My first ascension
> had the first two unvoluntarily fulfilled (no /oPoly in the entire
> game),

Chances are quite good that you'll find a potion of polymorph during
the course of a game, and these are difficult to identify without
either burning an identify spell or polymorphing yourself or an item.

> so I don't know if there is any class-conduct interdependence.
> Maybe wizards with starting MR are best here?

Yep. When i break the polyselfless conduct, that's almost always due
to blundering into a polymorph trap at unawares; chances of avoiding
that are best for wizards, and slightly improved for characters with
automatic searching - rogues, archeologists, tourists, characters
wielding Excalibur.

